{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vh2Frh6ibWL3xuFCRvN5Fp","type":"tool_result","content":"2544:### 5.4 Figure\n2554:### 5.5 Tables, in full\n2877:## 6. The forest regime, on paper (item 06, 2026-11-01)\n2929:### 6.1 Statements\n2984:### 6.2 Proofs\n3181:### 6.3 The brute-force check\n3287:### 6.4 Findings\n3330:### 6.5 Lean-ready statements for item 11\n3363:### 6.6 Kill verdict\n3370:### 6.7 What this does not claim\n3393:### 6.8 Tables, in full\n3419:## 7. `β⁺` on paper: the provable constructions analysed (item 07, 2026-11-01)\n3464:### 7.1 The layer lemma (deterministic)\n3497:### 7.2 The exploration recursion for the layer fractions\n3578:### 7.3 Greedy independent sets, and the slot picture\n3641:### 7.4 The recursions against simulation\n3713:### 7.5 `β⁺(d, μ)` on the uniform model, beside `β*`, `b` and the rule\n3752:### 7.6 Findings\n3827:### 7.7 Tables, in full\n3938:### 7.8 Kill verdict\n3952:### 7.9 What this does not claim\n3981:## 8. The structured count: can a richer first moment lift `β*`? (item 08, 2026-11-01)\n4012:### 8.1 The structure, and that a layout forces it (checked)\n4068:### 8.2 `β*_ℓ` at equal spacing, `ℓ ≤ 4`\n4104:### 8.3 Every spacing, not just equal spacing (`ℓ = 2`), and the limit\n4164:### 8.4 The finite floors on the 200 Chu & Stuckey instances (`ℓ = 2`)\n4242:### 8.5 The diagnostic: where the slack is\n4357:### 8.6 Findings\n4390:### 8.7 Kill verdict\n4399:### 8.8 What this does not claim\n====\n4. **One free half-stack.** The single pair at the exact size `|R| = n −\n   p − k` (the Lean statement) is a different valid bound from the old\n   `|R| = t − 1`; on the 200 instances its floor is higher on 105 and lower\n   on none (0.5 stacks on average), with the same zero violations at 0.01.\n   It is a rounding, not a structure.\n5. **The balanced position is optimal for the single pair**, exactly, on\n   97-point grids at `β* ± 0.02` (§8.3) — the scan of §2 and Appendix E\n   lost nothing to the choice of `t`.\n\n### 8.7 Kill verdict\n\n**Met.** `β*_ℓ(6, 1) − β*(6, 1) = −0.051, −0.085, −0.111` for `ℓ = 2, 3, 4`\nat equal spacing, all below 0.03 — below zero; and no spacing does\nbetter (§8.3). The structured count does not pay, and the diagnostic says\nwhere the slack is: in the union bound, by clustering (finding 3). No\ncertified value is wrong, no theorem is false, no published claim of ours\ndrifts; the record's zero-of-200 at the 0.01 floor is reproduced.\n\n### 8.8 What this does not claim\n\n- **The constant.** A first moment is a lower bound on the truth whatever\n  `ℓ`; `β*` stays the best proved constant and the measured `opt / n` stays\n  0.06–0.10 above it at 125 × 125.\n- **That no richer first moment can lift `β*`.** What is shown is that\n  *this* family — more nested pairs, any spacing, any overlap — cannot,\n  and why (finding 2). A count over cluster representatives (one canonical\n  pair per connected family of empty pairs, as in the second-moment method\n  with conditioning) is the kind that could, and is not attempted.\n- **That the second moment locates the slack by itself.** `E[X²]/E[X]²`\n  exponentially large is consistent with the union bound being loose and\n  with it being tight on a rare event; it is (b), the deterministic\n  measurement on certified instances, that attributes the slack, and (b)\n  reaches 75 customers, not 125.\n- **Exactness of Harper's bound.** Under a cap it is a lower bound on the\n  true Harper value; the inequalities in §8.5 (b) are stated in the\n  direction that survives that.\n- **Anything about the padded generators.** Every exponent and the second\n  moment are for the uniform cover model given sizes; the floors on the\n  200 instances use their empirical sizes in that model, as Appendix E\n  does; item 10 is where the generator's validity is settled.\n- **The literature.** The nested-pair count and its failure by\n  `ε log(1/ε)` are, as far as we found, not stated anywhere; the clustering\n  failure of the plain second moment for first-moment thresholds is a\n  standard phenomenon on `G(n, p)` and is not claimed as new. \"We found no\n  prior statement\" is all that is claimed.\n====FIG\ndef figure_opt(rule: pd.DataFrame, out: Path = FIGURE_OPT) -> Path:\n    \"\"\"Small multiples, one per product ratio `r`, for the constant-e series\n    (the fixed generator and the Chu & Stuckey classes; a Bernoulli series at\n    fixed `p` has `e` growing with `n` and is left out): against the series'\n    realised `e`, the certified slope `b` with its 2-se bar, the rule's\n    `width / n` at the largest `n`, and `beta*` at the realised sizes; one\n    marker per series, circles the fixed generator, squares the Chu & Stuckey\n    classes. Three quantities, three categorical slots; PNG for the report.\"\"\"\n    import matplotlib\n    matplotlib.use(\"Agg\")\n    import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n    from matplotlib.lines import Line2D\n\n    slopes = series_slopes(rule)\n    slopes = slopes[slopes[\"constant e\"]]\n    ratios = sorted(set(slopes[\"r\"]), key=lambda r: RATIO_LABELS_INV[r])\n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, len(ratios), figsize=(3.4 * len(ratios), 3.4), sharey=True, squeeze=False)\n    markers = {\"fixed\": \"o\", \"chu_stuckey\": \"s\", \"bernoulli\": \"D\"}\n    for ax, rl in zip(axes.ravel(), ratios):\n        g = slopes[slopes[\"r\"] == rl]\n        ax.axvline(1.0, color=\"#d8d7d2\", lw=1, zorder=0)\n        ax.axvspan(2.0, 2.4, color=\"#f1f0eb\", zorder=0)\n        for gen, s in g.groupby(\"generator\"):\n            mk = markers[gen]\n            ax.errorbar(s[\"e\"], s[\"b\"], yerr=2 * s[\"se b\"], fmt=\"none\", ecolor=QUANTITY_COLOURS[\"b (certified slope)\"],\n                        elinewidth=1, zorder=2)\n            for key, col in QUANTITY_COLOURS.items():\n                y = {\"b (certified slope)\": \"b\", \"rule / n at largest n\": \"rule/n at max n\",\n                     \"beta* (first moment)\": \"beta* (sizes)\"}[key]\n                ax.plot(s[\"e\"], s[y], ls=\"none\", marker=mk, ms=6, color=col, zorder=3)\n        ax.set_title(f\"r = m / n = {rl}\", fontsize=10, color=\"#0b0b0b\", loc=\"left\")\n        ax.set_xlim(0, float(g[\"e\"].max()) * 1.08)\n        ax.set_ylim(-0.02, 1.0)\n        ax.grid(False)\n        for side in (\"top\", \"right\"):\n            ax.spines[side].set_visible(False)\n        for side in (\"left\", \"bottom\"):\n            ax.spines[side].set_color(\"#c3c2b7\")\n        ax.tick_params(colors=\"#52514e\", labelsize=8)\n        ax.set_xlabel(\"realised excess e\", fontsize=9, color=\"#52514e\")\n    axes[0, 0].set_ylabel(\"per customer\", fontsize=9, color=\"#52514e\")\n    handles = [Line2D([], [], ls=\"none\", marker=\"o\", color=c, ms=6, label=k) for k, c in QUANTITY_COLOURS.items()]\n    handles += [Line2D([], [], ls=\"none\", marker=\"o\", color=\"#52514e\", ms=6, label=\"fixed generator, n = 30–100\"),\n                Line2D([], [], ls=\"none\", marker=\"s\", color=\"#52514e\", ms=6, label=\"Chu & Stuckey classes, n = 30–125\")]\n    fig.legend(handles=handles, loc=\"upper right\", frameon=False, fontsize=8, ncol=2)\n    fig.text(0.01, 0.95, \"grey line: e = 1, where the first moment turns on; band: e = 2–2.4, the hardness ridge; \"\n             \"bars: ± 2 se on b\", fontsize=8, color=\"#52514e\")\n    fig.tight_layout(rect=(0, 0, 1, 0.86))\n    out.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n    fig.savefig(out, dpi=150, facecolor=\"#fcfcfb\")\n    plt.close(fig)\n    return out\n\n\n# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7a8ec96c-60f9-4968-b844-122ae8421af8","uuid":"d1f3ca37-66f1-4451-bdda-1d635197c1fa","timestamp":"2026-11-01T05:23:55.165Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"2544:### 5.4 Figure\n2554:### 5.5 Tables, in full\n2877:## 6. The forest regime, on paper (item 06, 2026-11-01)\n2929:### 6.1 Statements\n2984:### 6.2 Proofs\n3181:### 6.3 The brute-force che