{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CgbhEThrGih23x71YngkF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MfHpsmFG5MxPRHtnVRgFsj","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport pandas as pd, numpy as np\nfrom learning.excess_ridge import lattice_cells, lattice_series\nf = pd.read_csv(\"learning/data/excess_ridge_lattice.csv\")\nc = lattice_cells(f); s = lattice_series(c, f)\ncell_md = c[[\"generator\",\"m\",\"param\",\"col_mean\",\"e\",\"opt_n\",\"connected\",\"median_log10_nodes\",\"median_log10_reachable\",\n             \"median_log10_count_closing\",\"median_log10_share_closing\",\"median_log10_count_search\",\"audit_ok\"]].rename(\n    columns={\"median_log10_nodes\":\"med log10 nodes\",\"median_log10_reachable\":\"med log10 reachable (§36)\",\n             \"median_log10_count_closing\":\"med log10 #optimal closing orders\",\"median_log10_share_closing\":\"med log10 share of 20!\",\n             \"median_log10_count_search\":\"med log10 #orders, search measure\",\"audit_ok\":\"audit ok (of 150)\"}).to_markdown(index=False, floatfmt=\".2f\")\nser_md = s[[\"generator\",\"m\",\"nodes_peak_col_mean\",\"nodes_peak_e\",\"count_closing_shape\",\"count_closing_peak_col_mean\",\"count_closing_rho_cells\",\n            \"reachable_shape\",\"reachable_rho_cells\",\"count_closing_rho_instances\",\"count_search_rho_instances\",\"opt_n_rho_instances\",\"share_rho_opt_n\"]].rename(\n    columns={\"nodes_peak_col_mean\":\"node peak col_mean\",\"nodes_peak_e\":\"node peak e\",\"count_closing_shape\":\"#optimal orders: shape\",\n             \"count_closing_peak_col_mean\":\"its max at col_mean\",\"count_closing_rho_cells\":\"ρ(cells) with nodes\",\"reachable_shape\":\"reachable (§36): shape\",\n             \"reachable_rho_cells\":\"its ρ(cells)\",\"count_closing_rho_instances\":\"ρ(inst) #orders, nodes\",\"count_search_rho_instances\":\"ρ(inst) search-measure count, nodes\",\n             \"opt_n_rho_instances\":\"ρ(inst) opt/n, nodes\",\"share_rho_opt_n\":\"ρ(inst) share, opt/n\"}).to_markdown(index=False, floatfmt=\".3f\")\ntext = f\"\"\"### 9.4 The secondary: optimal closing orders at `n = 20`\n\nOn the 5,400 certified campaign instances at `n = 20` (`m ∈ {{20, 40}}`,\nboth generators, 9 densities each, 150 per cell), the number of closing\norders that realise the certified optimum under the construction value, and\nunder Chu & Stuckey's search measure, counted exactly by a vectorised path\ncount over the `2^20` closed-customer sets (`lattice_weights`,\n`lattice_count_np`; the same two edge costs as `learning.degeneracy`, equal\nto it on every edge, minimum and count of 40 random small instances in the\ntests). Instances regenerated from their seeds (`n_ones` asserted); 3.44\ncore-hours of lattice time (2.3 s per instance, max 5.0), 26 min wall on 8\nworkers at nice 19, load 8; §36's reachable-set counts joined from\n`cover_excess_states.csv` for the same instances.\n\n**Audit, free and new.** The lattice minimum under the construction value\nand under the search measure equals the certified optimum on **5,400 of\n5,400** — §8's lattice audit, which shared no code with the search, reached\n`n ≤ 15`; this extends it to every campaign instance at 20 with an\nindependent second implementation. No certified value is wrong.\n\n**The count is monotone, not peaked** — as §8's `ρ = 0.70` with `opt / n`\npredicted. Along every series the median `log10` count rises with density\nto `log10 20! = 18.39` where the graph is complete and every order is\noptimal; on the Bernoulli grids it dips at the sparse end only because\ndisconnected instances multiply their components' counts (on connected\ninstances alone the minimum is at the sparsest connected cell on all four\nseries, and the shape is increasing). Spearman with the median log nodes\nacross cells: −0.77 to −0.98; per instance −0.54 to −0.91; the share of\n`20!` tracks `opt / n` at `ρ = 0.69` pooled (0.70 at `n ≤ 15`, §8). The\nsearch-measure count moves with the construction count (`ρ = 0.93`) and\nis the larger on 38% of instances. No instance has a unique optimal order\nup to twins (0 of 5,400, as 0 of 2,812 at `n ≤ 15`); 919 are complete\ngraphs. §36's reachable count, on the same instances, peaks where the nodes\npeak on the fixed `m = n` series and is decreasing on the other three grids\nwhose node peak is at the sparse edge — the one quantity on this table that\nmoves *with* the nodes. So the ridge is where the optimum is *least*\ndegenerate among connected instances of its size only in the sense that\ndegeneracy grows monotonically with density; nothing in the count peaks.\n\nPer series:\n\n{ser_md}\n\nPer cell:\n\n{cell_md}\n\"\"\"\np = Path(\"reports/excess_phase_diagram.md\"); s_ = p.read_text()\nassert \"<!-- LATTICE -->\" in s_\np.write_text(s_.replace(\"<!-- LATTICE -->\", text))\nprint(\"section 9.4 written\")\nEOF\ncat >> Ralph_Loops/loop0010/PROGRESS.md <<'EOF'\n\n---\n\n## Iteration 9 — 2026-11-01 05:20\n\nItem 09, the ridge mechanism: one hypothesis, one test.\n\n### Completed\n\n- **`learning/excess_ridge.py`** (stages `ridge`, `ridge-lattice`,\n  `ridge-tables`, `figure-ridge` of `learning.excess`): for §25's 25 series\n  at `n ∈ {50, 60, 75}` (9,497 certified instances, 190 cells, through\n  `ridge_theory.load_frames` so the node peaks are §25's to the digit) the\n  two finite floors per instance from item 02's size histograms, `β*` per\n  cell from the pooled histogram, the two gaps, their cell means with\n  standard errors, each gap's peak by §25 (b)'s parabola with a bootstrap\n  interval over instances, §25 (d)'s score against the node peak and its\n  interval, the kill, and the profile shapes; a vectorised lattice over the\n  `2^20` closed-customer sets (both edge costs of `learning.degeneracy`,\n  equal to it on every edge, minimum and count in the tests) for the\n  secondary. `learning/data/excess_ridge_{instances,cells