{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cgbi1qAgyzAfKmGJ2cp9Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 09 is done; four items remain unchecked, so no `LOOP_COMPLETE`.\n\n**Result: H1 is dead on both readings of the floor.** The first-moment gap's peak lies outside the node peak's bootstrap interval on 22 of 25 series (finite floor) and 23 of 25 (`β*`), against the 12 the kill allows. Mean scores are 0.109 and 0.135 decades. The miss is one-sided: the gap's maximum sits on the dense side of the ridge, at excess 2.2–5.3 against the ridge's 1.8–3.0, and its location is less constant across product ratios than the excess itself. What survives is weaker: the gap is a broad plateau over `e ≈ 2–5`, and at the ridge it is within one stack per 50 customers of its own maximum on every series. The gap's height also orders the ratios backwards from the ridge's height, so it cannot be what sets the ridge.\n\n**Secondary at `n = 20`.** The number of optimal closing orders is monotone increasing in density on all four series (connected instances), Spearman −0.77 to −0.98 with the node count across cells, as §8's correlation with `opt / n` predicted. Nothing in the count peaks.\n\n**Two free audits.** The 0.01 floor is never above the optimum on all 9,497 certified random covers at 50–75. The lattice minimum under both measures equals the certified optimum on 5,400 of 5,400 campaign instances at `n = 20`, extending §8's code-independent audit from 15 to 20 customers.\n\n**Deliverables.**\n- `learning/excess_ridge.py` with stages `ridge`, `ridge-lattice`, `ridge-tables`, `figure-ridge` in `learning.excess`; a vectorised `2^20` closing-order lattice checked against `learning.degeneracy` on every edge.\n- `tests/test_excess_ridge.py`, 10 tests.\n- `reports/excess_phase_diagram.md` §9 with every table, `reports/figures/excess_ridge.png`, scratch logs and tables under `reports/scratch/excess_ridge/`.\n- PROGRESS.md entry 